## 4. End-to-end operational workflows

### A. Staff access

1. A provisioned staff member opens `/activate`, supplies staff email, four-digit PIN, new password and confirmation.
2. On successful activation, the staff member signs in on `/login` with email/password.
3. The web app's auth/session state is passed to the shell (name, email, role and organization identifier). Sign-out returns to login.
4. Backend auth validates session expiry and active-account state. User role and organization context are server-derived; access is not intended to trust organization IDs from the browser.

### B. Patient visit and care plan

1. Register a patient (or select an existing patient from the patient directory/record).
2. Create and schedule an appointment by patient, provider, date/time and visit details. API checks patient/provider eligibility and schedule overlap.
3. Open the appointment and start a consultation. The encounter is tied to the appointment; creating it transitions a scheduled/confirmed appointment to `IN_PROGRESS`.
4. Record chief complaint, clinical notes and vital signs. Complete the encounter when finished; completed consultations are read-only.
5. Create/update an Ayurvedic assessment for that consultation, then finalize it. Finalization marks the assessment complete and locks subsequent edits.
6. Create a treatment plan linked to the assessment/consultation. Add plan items, then activate the plan when the assessment is complete and the plan has at least one item. Plan status/actions indicate draft, active, completed or cancelled.
7. From a plan or consultation, proceed to Panchakarma course and/or prescription workflows where clinically appropriate.

The UI provides these links/actions in appointment detail, consultation detail, patient history, and treatment-plan detail; do not model them as a single wizard unless intentionally changing the existing UX.

### C. Panchakarma therapy

1. Create a course connected to an active treatment plan (or select a consultation where the form permits).
2. Schedule sessions and assign therapist/date/time.
3. Use the daily therapist queue for start, complete, cancel or no-show actions; record response/tolerance in the relevant dialogs.
4. The API checks therapist scheduling conflicts and enforces course/session lifecycle actions.

### D. Prescription and dispensing

1. Author a prescription linked to the patient/consultation and optionally a treatment plan; save draft or activate.
2. Queue/prepare a dispensation from its prescription.
3. Start and complete or cancel the dispensation. On completion, API consumes suitable stock batches using FEFO and records movements.
4. Use prescription and dispensation details to review the prescription, fulfillment items and status.

### E. Invoice and payment

1. Create an invoice for a patient and add priced line items, discounts/taxes as applicable.
2. Issue the invoice; review item details and payment allocations.
3. Record payment(s), allocate them to one or more invoices, and view/print receipt.
4. If authorized, void a payment; the server recalculates affected invoice balances atomically. Paid invoices and voided payments are not ordinary editable/deletable records.

### F. Reports, configuration, and oversight

- Reports filter by date range and report domain; CSV export is available through the report API. Financial and other sensitive report access is role-dependent.
- Admin/Super Admin users manage organization profile, staff, role, activation, audit logs, and sequence configuration.
- All users have profile/security views; audit entries are read-only records.

## 5. Domain model and API surface

All API paths below are relative to `/api/v1`. Successful responses use an envelope `{ success, data, timestamp }`; error responses have status/message/error/timestamp/path fields and may include validation details. DTO validation transforms input, whitelists declared fields, and rejects non-whitelisted properties.

| Module | Main records and operations |
|---|---|
| Auth/Health | `GET /health`; `GET /auth/me`; `POST /auth/activate`; Better Auth session/cookie or bearer token authentication. |
| Patients | `POST/GET /patients`, `GET /patients/:id`, `PATCH /patients/:id`; paginated/searchable registry and profile/status maintenance. |
| Appointments | Create/list/today/providers/detail/update/cancel under `/appointments`; schedule conflict checks; explicit cancellation. |
| Consultations | Create/list/detail/update/complete/cancel under `/consultations`; links encounter to appointment. |
| Assessments | Create/list/detail/update/finalize under `/assessments`; unique per consultation and finalization lock. |
| Treatment plans | Create/list/detail/update and activate/complete/cancel under `/treatment-plans`; items, consultation and patient history. |
| Panchakarma | Course and session create/list/detail/update; schedule/start/complete/cancel/no-show actions; daily queue and patient history. |
| Pharmacy | Prescriptions, dispensations, medicine stock, stock adjustments, stock movements, and queue; complete dispensation consumes batches. |
| Billing | Summary, invoices, invoice items, issue/cancel, payments, allocations, receipt and payment void. |
| Reports | Summary/executive summary, patients, appointments, clinical, treatments, Panchakarma, pharmacy, financial, providers, daily; CSV export. |
| Settings | Overview, organization, staff, roles, activation/PIN, audit logs, configuration, profile and security/session summary. |

Primary data chain:

`Organization → Patient → Appointment → Consultation → Ayurvedic Assessment → Treatment Plan + Items → Panchakarma Course / Sessions and Prescription → Dispensation / Inventory → Invoice / Payment`

These are related records, not all mandatory sequential steps for every patient. In particular, pharmacy, Panchakarma and billing can be separate operational branches. Core models live in [`schema.prisma`](../apps/api/prisma/schema.prisma); endpoint/controller contracts are in each module under [`apps/api/src/`](../apps/api/src/).

## 6. Roles, visibility, and operational constraints

Roles in the system include `SUPER_ADMIN`, `ADMIN`, `DOCTOR`, `THERAPIST`, `PHARMACIST`, `RECEPTIONIST`, and `ACCOUNTANT`.

- Sidebar currently lists the same destinations without role filtering. Settings and report tabs have client-side role-based visibility as described above.
- Server API authorization is authoritative. Controller actions declare role requirements; `SUPER_ADMIN` bypasses role lists, but organization-scoped services still require tenant context where applicable.
- Broad API role pattern: clinical write actions are for admin/super-admin/doctor/therapist (some appointment writes also allow reception); pharmacy execution/inventory writes are pharmacist/admin; billing writes are accountant/reception/admin, and payment void is narrower. Individual routes may differ; see controller decorators.
- Patients, appointments, consultations, assessments, plans, therapy, prescriptions, dispensations, invoices and payments are organization-scoped. The organization context comes from the authenticated user/session.
- Scheduling checks appointment overlap; therapy sessions check therapist overlap. Terminal clinical/financial states have explicit lock/void/cancel behavior.
- Clinic timezone defaults to `Asia/Kolkata`. API date-range utilities use clinic-local day boundaries for operational dates.

